PCSX2 with a native RTX Remix render backend
============================================

This build submits PlayStation 2 geometry to the RTX Remix runtime through the
Remix API, so a PS2 title is path traced rather than rasterised. It is a research
build, not a product. Read "What works" before filing anything.

Source and full documentation:
  https://github.com/BRAGme/pcsx2-rtx-remix


TWO THINGS ARE NOT IN THIS ZIP AND IT WILL NOT RUN WITHOUT THEM
---------------------------------------------------------------

1. A REMIX RUNTIME ON THE EXTENDED API LINE.

   This backend does NOT work with the stock NVIDIA RTX Remix runtime, for two
   independent reasons: the version handshake rejects it (the extended line
   reserves REMIXAPI_VERSION_MINOR = 1000, stock is on the 0.6.x line), and stock
   has no remixapi_CreateTexture -- which this backend needs, because PS2 textures
   are decoded out of emulated memory and there is no file on disk to point at.

   Known-good source:
     https://github.com/RemixProjGroup/dxvk-remix   (maintainer: Kim2091)
     Release tag: remix-plus-1.5.1
     Asset:       Remix_Plus_v1.5.1_x64_games_release.zip

   HONEST NOTE ON WHICH RUNTIME WAS ACTUALLY TESTED. The measurements quoted in
   this file and in the README were taken against a numos3 build of dxvk-remix
   (it reports "dxvk-remix (remix-main+abbae23d)"), not against the 1.5.1 tag
   above. Both sit on the same API line -- numos3's header is 0.1000.1, this
   build's is 0.1000.0, and the handshake only breaks on the minor -- so either
   should connect. But only the numos3 build has actually run this backend for any
   length of time. Treat 1.5.1 as expected-to-work rather than verified, and if
   you see behaviour this file does not describe, the runtime is the first
   variable to change.

   Unzip it into a subfolder called  remix\  next to pcsx2-qtx64.exe:

     pcsx2-qtx64.exe
     remix\d3d9.dll
     remix\...

   DO NOT put d3d9.dll directly beside the executable. Anything else in the
   process that resolves that name would pick it up. The subfolder is mandatory,
   not a convention. You can also set PCSX2_REMIX_DLL to a full path, or point
   RuntimePath under [Remix] in the settings at one.

   It is not bundled here because that folder contains third-party
   redistributables (nvngx_dlss*.dll, cudart64_13.dll, GFSDK_Aftermath,
   libxess.dll, amd_fidelityfx_vk.dll) with their own terms, inside someone
   else's fork build. Get it from the people who made it.

2. A PS2 BIOS. Same as stock PCSX2 -- dump it from a console you own. No BIOS,
   games or keys are included or ever will be.


ENABLING THE BACKEND
--------------------

  Settings -> Graphics -> Renderer -> RTX Remix

There is also a Settings -> RTX Remix page for the backend's own knobs. Every knob
also has a PCSX2_REMIX_* environment variable, and an environment variable that is
already set always beats both the GUI and the per-game file -- deliberately, so an
A/B harness stays authoritative. The full table is in docs/Remix/KNOBS.md in the
repository.

Per-game settings go in <serial>.conf next to the exe. Six ship here. Two are
curated from measurements taken on those titles:

  SCUS-97545.conf  SOCOM: Combined Assault
  SLUS-20883.conf  Rainbow Six 3

The other four are transplants and say so in their own headers -- they carry the
engine-independent fixes and nothing that was guessed:

  SCUS-97134.conf  SOCOM: U.S. Navy SEALs   (sky and MINRT measured here)
  SCUS-97275.conf  SOCOM II                 (transplanted, unmeasured)
  SCUS-97474.conf  SOCOM 3                  (starter profile, unmeasured)
  SCUS-97399.conf  God of War               (starter profile, different engine)

They are worth reading even if you do not play those games: they record why each
setting is what it is, with the measurements behind it. None of them arms a
diagnostic.

Runtime-generated per-game state goes to RemixGames\<serial>\.


THIS BUILD IS PORTABLE
----------------------

portable.ini is present, so settings, memory cards and save states live in this
folder rather than Documents\PCSX2. That is deliberate: it means installing this
next to a normal PCSX2 cannot overwrite that install's configuration. Delete
portable.ini if you want the usual behaviour.

updater.exe is also deliberately absent. It updates to upstream PCSX2 releases,
which would overwrite this build with one that has no Remix backend in it.


WHAT WORKS, AND WHAT DOES NOT
-----------------------------

Seen working:
  Ghost Recon 2 (SLUS-21105)  -- world geometry, albedo textures, generated
                                 vertex normals, path-traced lighting. NOT
                                 re-verified since: that capture is 41 commits
                                 back and nobody has booted it since. Nothing is
                                 known to be wrong with it, but the blend-enum
                                 fix and the sprite-text gate both landed after
                                 it and both change what every title submits.
  Rainbow Six 3 (SLUS-20883)  -- the above plus character models
  SOCOM: Combined Assault (SCUS-97545) -- NOT playable yet. Geometry, textures and
                                 characters were verified 2026-08-02, but that is 83
                                 commits back and it has not been re-measured since.
                                 It is actively being worked on -- 18 of those commits
                                 touch this title -- but the device loss below still
                                 ends a session early. Do not download this expecting
                                 to play SOCOM.

Fixed since the first preview build:

  - THE FLAT-ALBEDO DEFECT WAS AN ENUM BUG, not texture binding. D3D9 enum values
    were being written into fields the runtime casts straight to Vulkan enums, so
    the backend asked for vertex colour and ignored the texture. MODULATE also
    read as Modulate2x, doubling surface brightness. Affects every title.

  - THE 1-IN-3 "FLICKERING TRIANGLES" was an empty present window, not depth
    precision. A game rendering below the vsync rate submits zero geometry on
    every Nth presented window. PCSX2_REMIX_HOLDEMPTY re-presents the previous
    window; the period-3 signature is gone, offcadence 0 over 14,098 holds.
    Confirmed on Combined Assault and SOCOM 1.

  - PS2 TEXT IS DRAWN AS SPRITES, and the first classification gate discarded all
    of it before the UI classifier could see it.

  - USD CAPTURE was broken for every game by a locale bug: PCSX2 sets a grouping
    locale, the runtime builds prim names with a bare stringstream, and hex hashes
    came out comma-separated -- invalid USD identifiers. Every capture failed
    18 ms in. Captures now work.

  - ON RAINBOW SIX 3 THE CAMERA IS READ OUT OF EE MEMORY rather than recovered
    from VU1, which sidesteps the candidate-set problem entirely for that title.
    Its shadow pass is dropped, its baked lightmaps are folded in, and the
    synthetic projection was calibrated against a physical target for the first
    time (lamp-to-fixture residual 14.0 units at fov 60, 1.4 at fov 70).

Known blockers, in order of how much they will bother you:

  - ONE CAMERA PER FRAME, applied to every draw. The backend picks a single
    view-projection from the frame's candidates and uses it for the whole frame;
    per-draw camera association is not built. On Rainbow Six 3 save state 9,
    99.84% of frames anchor to world space -- but on save state 7, which runs a
    different VU1 microprogram, three runs measured 35.3%, 48.4% and 68.3%. The
    rest fall back to view space and geometry welds to the screen.

    Partly superseded on Rainbow Six 3 only, by the EE-memory camera above. Every
    other title still uses the VU1 recovery path.

  - REPRODUCIBLE DEVICE LOSS (0x60D0DEAD) on some titles. On SOCOM the hang lands
    within a fraction of a second of the renderer going live. Not tuneable from
    the outside.

  - VERTEX EXPLOSIONS on some draws -- white shards across the frame.
    PCSX2_REMIX_POSLIMIT is a blunt mitigation; the decode is not fully solved.

  - Only the titles above have been measured. The four transplanted .conf files
    have NOT been tested on their own titles. Anything else is untested, which is
    not the same as broken.

The sibling project, RPCS3 with the same kind of backend, is further along:
  https://github.com/BRAGme/rpcs3-rtx


BUILD PROVENANCE
----------------

  pcsx2-qtx64.exe built from  BRAGme/pcsx2-rtx-remix  branch remix-backend,
  commit @GIT_SHORT@. It reports @GIT_REV@ in Help -> About, so you can check.

PCSX2 is GPL-3.0. This is a modified fork; the complete corresponding source is
the repository linked at the top.
